        <name>Daniel Mietchen</name>
        <address>Jena Thuringia Germany</address>
        <description><![CDATA[Contact email: daniel.mietchen@ibmt.fraunhofer.de<br>City: Jena<br>State or Province: Thuringia<br>Country: Germany<br>Science degree / education?: Yes<br>Basic lab skills?: Yes<br>Lab space?: No<br>Other info: My background is in biophysics, with little overlap to vaccine development.<br><br>I have a strong interest and broad experience in data sharing in public health emergencies [1] as well as in open science more broadly [2] and in covering science and medicine-related topics via the Wikimedia ecosystem [3].<br><br>I would thus like to assist RaDVaC from those angles.<br><br>A good way to reach me is via https://twitter.com/EvoMRI .<br><br>Some pointers to relevant activities I was involved in:<br>[1]<br>- Open drug discovery for the Zika virus. https://doi.org/10.12688/f1000research.8013.1<br>- Progress in promoting data sharing in public health emergencies. https://dx.doi.org/10.2471/BLT.17.192096<br>- Fostering global data sharing. https://wellcomeopenresearch.org/articles/5-267/v1<br>[2]<br>- The transformative nature of transparency in research funding. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002027<br>- Publishing the research process. https://doi.org/10.3897/rio.1.e7547<br>- Open science in practice. https://doi.org/10.3897/rio.7.e68595<br>[3]<br>- Wikipedia as a gateway to biomedical research. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0190046<br>- Representing COVID-19 information in collaborative knowledge graphs: the case of Wikidata. http://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4549834<br>- Developing a scalable framework for partnerships between health agencies and the Wikimedia ecosystem.  https://doi.org/10.3897/arphapreprints.e68129]]></description>
